e": "question"}], "id": 0}], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "Declaring PHP variables", "type": "text"}], "type": "instructions"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Take Away", "type": "group_name"}], "type": "group"}, {"content": {"incorrect_feedback": [{"content": "Whoops! Something important is missing.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Maybe next time...", "type": "text"}], "allowed_attempts": 2, "correct_feedback": "Correct!", "attempt_feedback": [{"content": "", "type": "text"}, {"content": "", "type": "text"}]}, "type": "feedback"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Reading", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Writing", "type": "skill"}, {"content": "Speaking", "type": "skill"}], "type": "learning_skills"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Instructions: Complete the following steps. Fill in the Self-Assessment.", "type": "text"}], "type": "post_instructions"}], "id": 11, "type": "DWS"}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": true, "type": "has_nav"}, {"content": true, "type": "has_exercise_controls"}], "type": "lab_options"}], "stimuli": [{"_data": [], "contents": [{}], "template_override": 1, "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "contents": [{"value": {"coverimg": "enpe7n6le02ob03re2img01.jpg", "images": [{"time": "0", "text": "<b>George</b>: Mary, could you give me a hand, please? <br>\n<b>Mary</b>: Sure! But, you are making that variable wrong. The name of variables cannot be followed by numbers. Don't you remember that Mr. Williams told us that the sign $ cannot be changed in the PHP variables?<br>\n<b>George</b>:  Who? <br>\n<b>Mary</b>: The engineer, who came last week for the training session.  <br>\n<b>George</b>: No. I wasn't there. What did he say?<br>", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob03re2img01.jpg", "id": 0}, {"time": "30000", "text": "<b>Mary</b>: First, variables are containers that help us to store data. A variable is created when you assign a value to a text. It's important to consider that variables work differently in each programming language. In this case, PHP doesn't have a command which can declare a variable. In addition, it's a must that all variables start with the $ sign, and remember that variables are case-sensitive, too. We can put short names, just a letter works, but we can also set long names. Letters or numbers different from A-Z and 0-9 can't be used to create those names, right?", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob03re2img02.jpg", "id": 1}, {"time": "75000", "text": "<b>George</b>: Yes! And, what about scopes? <br>\n<b>Mary</b>: Scopes are the parts of scripts, in which a variable is used or referenced. There are three scopes. Do you know them?<br>\n<b>George</b>: Yes, but I only understand two of them. Firstly, the global scope, which is used when a variable is declared outside a function. For this reason, it can't be accessed from within a function, only from the outside. Secondly, the local scope works the other way around. These scopes can only be accessed from within functions. <br>\n<b>Mary</b>: You've got it! The last one, static scope is used when you don't want the data to be deleted after the function is executed.ng.", "image": "enpe7n6le02ob03re2img03.jpg", "id": 2}], "audiosrc": "enpe7n6le02ob03re2aud01"}, "type": "slideshow"}], "title": "", "id": 1, "type": "audio"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "contents": [{"value": "01.jpg", "type": "img"}, {"value": "02.jpg", "type": "img"}, {"value": "03.jpg", "type": "img"}], "title": "", "id": 2, "type": "static"}], "id": 0, "type": "scholar"}]}